1
00:00:02,150 --> 00:00:07,704
When I first started learning Azure, I mainly used Microsoft Learn and the documentation.

2
00:00:07,704 --> 00:00:09,688
They gave me useful concepts.

3
00:00:09,688 --> 00:00:14,846
But as a beginner, I found it harder to connect those concepts together.

4
00:00:16,150 --> 00:00:20,263
I understood App Service, SQL, and caching on their own.

5
00:00:20,263 --> 00:00:23,554
But when traffic increased, what should I change?

6
00:00:23,554 --> 00:00:28,079
Would more App instances help, or was the database the problem?

7
00:00:28,079 --> 00:00:30,958
Those connections were less clear to me.

8
00:00:32,150 --> 00:00:33,682
So I built this.

9
00:00:33,682 --> 00:00:42,110
Stack and Survive is a small game where beginners can experience a few Azure architecture decisions, rather than just read their definitions.

10
00:00:44,150 --> 00:00:47,556
Traffic comes in, and you change the architecture.

11
00:00:47,556 --> 00:00:51,815
You can add capacity, introduce cache, or protect the edge.

12
00:00:51,815 --> 00:00:57,350
The game then shows what happens to availability, lost sales, and running cost.

13
00:00:59,150 --> 00:01:02,551
This is the part I care about.

14
00:01:02,551 --> 00:01:04,494
What would you change?

15
00:01:04,494 --> 00:01:05,951
More App instances?

16
00:01:05,951 --> 00:01:07,409
A stronger database?

17
00:01:07,409 --> 00:01:10,324
It isn't about clicking Scale Out.

18
00:01:10,324 --> 00:01:15,182
It's about noticing which layer is under pressure, and why.

19
00:01:16,150 --> 00:01:17,388
This run survived.

20
00:01:17,388 --> 00:01:19,863
But was it a good architecture?

21
00:01:19,863 --> 00:01:23,163
Maybe I added more capacity than I needed.

22
00:01:23,163 --> 00:01:29,350
Keeping the service available matters, but so does the cost of the choices I made.

23
00:01:30,150 --> 00:01:35,059
After the run, I want people to look back and try another choice.

24
00:01:35,059 --> 00:01:39,214
They can return to Microsoft Learn with a more concrete question.

25
00:01:39,214 --> 00:01:42,990
The idea is to play, observe, reflect, and try again.

26
00:01:45,150 --> 00:01:48,150
This is not a replacement for Microsoft Learn.

27
00:01:48,150 --> 00:01:57,150
I hope it gives customers a simpler, more enjoyable first step into Azure, and helps them feel more comfortable exploring the real services afterward.
